ac3.library and ac3fpu.library
==============================

A/52 (Dolby Digital, AC-3) audio, as found on DVDs, in transport streams
and in .ac3 files, decoded to 16-bit PCM: stereo, mono or Dolby Surround
compatible stereo, the stream's 5.1 mixed down as A/52 says. For any
program, as mpega.library is for MPEG audio. Made with AmigaMCE, but not
part of it.

  ac3.library     liba52's integer build: a 68020 or better, no FPU needed.
                  Not for a 68060 (its 64-bit multiplies would be emulated
                  by 68060.library): use ac3fpu.library there.
  ac3fpu.library  liba52's floating-point build, for a 68040 or 68060 with
                  its FPU (uses the 68040's single-precision instructions:
                  not for a 68881/68882).

Both have the same functions (ac3_lib.sfd, include/libraries/ac3.h,
ac3.doc). A program tries ac3fpu.library on a 68040/68060 with an FPU,
else ac3.library.

How fast (estimates)
--------------------

On a 68020 as vamos's Musashi core counts cycles, one frame (1536 samples
a channel: 32 ms at 48 kHz) takes about 3.5 million cycles in stereo at
192 kbit/s and 4.8 million in 5.1 at 448 kbit/s: about 110 and 150 MHz of
a 68020 to keep up. So a 68020 or 68030 cannot play AC-3 as it arrives;
a 68040 at 40 MHz or so can manage stereo; a 68060 plays 5.1 with time to
spare (ac3fpu.library). Not yet measured on real hardware.

Licence
-------

The libraries are free software under the GNU General Public License,
version 2 or (at your option) any later version, because liba52 is
(extras/liba52/COPYING). Their complete source is:

  extras/liba52/          liba52 0.8.0's decoder, and CHANGES (what was
                          changed for these libraries, and why)
  src/ac3lib/ac3lib.c     the library's functions
  src/ac3lib/port/        its config.h
  src/ac3lib/mktables.c   the build host's table maker
  src/sqlib/libinit.c, libinit.h, stabs.h   the library start-up
  the Makefile's ac3lib part

Whoever passes the libraries on (an Aminet archive, AmigaMCE's archive)
must pass this source on with them, or a written offer of it (GPL-2
section 3); putting it in the same archive is simplest. The GPL's text
goes in the archive too.

Their interface is MIT, (c) 2026 Brian King, and contains nothing of
liba52's: include/libraries/ac3.h, ac3_lib.sfd, the clib, inline and proto
headers made from it, and ac3.doc. A program compiles only these and
opens a library at run time, whatever its own licence. (The Free Software
Foundation counts a program and the libraries it loads as one work; that
is AmigaMCE's position for mpega and the ZZ9000's components too. Not legal
advice.)

Building: make ac3lib (build/ac3lib/ac3.library, ac3fpu.library);
make test-ac3 checks both the host's and a 68020's results against
tests/fixtures/ac3/expected.txt (tests/mkac3.sh made the fixtures).
